<p>James Reed Clark was born in Pennsylvania in October 1843 to Matthew Henderson Clark and Anne Alison Reed Clark. His parents married October 19, 1842. Anne died when James was five, and his father married Rebecca Marshall in 1850. That marriage added several children to the household, including David, Margaret M., Anne Alison, and Janet. Clark's father was a physician and employed one or two household servants.</p>

<p>Clark attended Washington &amp; Jefferson College in his hometown and trained as a pharmacist. He opened a drugstore in Washington, Pennsylvania, and later brought his younger brother David into the business.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/james-reed-clark/385-clark-mandan-drug-ad-1906.jpg" alt="Advertisement for Clark's Mandan drugstore in 1906">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>James married Laura Barlow Reed in 1868, and they settled near his parents in Washington. The prominent Reed family had deep roots in western Pennsylvania, and Laura was a distant relative of Clark's mother.</p>

<p>In 1883, shortly after completion of the Northern Pacific line to the West Coast, James and Laura moved their family to Mandan. Clark opened a drugstore at Fourth Avenue Northwest and Main Street. In 1885, Clark Drug Store moved into a new building completed by his future son-in-law, {% person "lyman-northrop-cary" %}Lyman Cary{% endperson %}.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/james-reed-clark/222-clark-james-portrait-photo.jpg" alt="Charcoal portrait of James Reed Clark">
    <figcaption>Charcoal portrait, 1946</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>James and Laura brought three children to Mandan: 13-year-old Colin Reed, 11-year-old Anne Alison, and nine-year-old Isabel J. A young German woman, Martha Jahuke, lived with the family as a domestic servant.</p>

<p>Laura's father, Alexander Reed, had served for years on the board of curators of his town's Citizens Library. James and Laura shared his interest in public libraries. Clark's store became the repository for a public lending collection and housed it until the books outgrew the space in 1903. The collection was a forerunner of today's Mandan Public Library.</p>

<p>Clark also served on a federal grand jury in Devils Lake in 1893 and on the Board of Trustees for the North Dakota State Reform School in Mandan from 1896 to 1897. An early elder of First Presbyterian Church, he was elected alternate principal lay commissioner for the North Dakota Presbytery in 1901.</p>

<p>Clark retired in 1906 at age 63 and sold his business to Olaf Lindelow, who renamed it Mandan Drug. James, Laura, and their youngest daughter Isabel returned to Washington, Pennsylvania, where James and Laura spent the rest of their lives.</p>

<p>Colin became a physician and received recognition for distinguished service during World War I. Isabel taught in Mandan and later Pennsylvania. Anne Alison married Lyman Cary in 1894 and remained in Mandan until her death in 1969.</p>

<p>The Society thanks Clark's great-great-granddaughter Lori Bettcher for genealogical information about Matthew, Anne Alison, and Nancy Clark.</p>
